<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SActionScript version="1" name="instrument_locate_continuity">

  <!-- Proposal 37 P3c, AC2: LOCATE WHILE STOPPED, THEN PLAY - AND THE NOTE
       THAT WAS ALREADY SOUNDING IS THERE, IN THE STATE IT WOULD HAVE BEEN IN.

       A locate while the transport is stopped demands nothing (D4: the barrier
       is not issued from setGlobalLocatorPos; requestSeek only runs while
       playing), so the whole burden falls on the run barrier issued at the next
       PLAY start plus the class-1 reposition protocol underneath it: the first
       page the readahead asks for does not start where the last one ended, so
       the processor RESETS, CHASES stateAt(P - K) and PRE-ROLLS K frames with
       the events at their real offsets before it renders a single audible
       sample, where

           K = min( max( 4096, tailFrames(), P - start(earliest held note) ),
                    4 s )

       Both halves are measured here, on the PLAYBACK path, through the capture
       backend - a render would not exercise the readahead's start at all.

       (a) PRESENCE - the chase. tw.test.clap.sine holds key 60 from 0 to 4 s.
       Locate to 96000 (2.0 s) while stopped, play, and 4096 frames of the
       recording, measured ONE DEVICE BLOCK IN, have to be that note. Without a
       chase the page would open with no voice at all and the estimator would be
       looking at silence; the energy assertion next to it says which of the two
       failures happened, and the onset assertion in front of both says whether
       the recording started where it was supposed to. The
       band is 261.626 +/- 3 Hz: a 4096-frame window is a coarse estimate and
       the note is either there at its own pitch or it is not there at all, so
       nothing is bought by making it tighter.

       (b) STATE - the pre-roll's reach-back. tw.native.303 holds key 84
       (1046.5 Hz) from 0 to 4 s with

           cutoff  = 20 Hz     (paramId 0, the floor)
           envMod  = 1.0       (paramId 2, the maximum)
           decay   = 2.0 s     (paramId 3)

       so the ONLY thing setting the filter cutoff is the decay envelope,
       sweeping 8020 Hz down to 20 Hz with a 2-second time constant. Two seconds
       in, a continuous run has env = exp(-1) = 0.368, i.e. a cutoff of 2963 Hz
       - still sweeping, and a third of the way down; the note has not converged
       and its sound at 2 s is a fact about the two seconds before it.

       At P = 65536 (the page the playhead at 96000 sits in) the reach-back
       gives K = min( max(4096, 96000, 65536), 192000 ) = 65536 = P, so the
       pre-roll starts at the note's own note-on and the envelope arrives at the
       page in the state a continuous run would have given it.

       MEASURED, and the band is that measurement +/- 10 %:

           full render,  frames [97024, 99072)   rms = 0.391949
           capture,      frames [ 1024,  3072)   rms = 0.391949   (identical)
           => band [0.352754, 0.431144]

       The two windows still agree to every digit, which is the property that
       makes the comparison worth making: the same material through the render
       and through the playback-capture path reads the same number.

       WHY BOTH WINDOWS START ONE DEVICE BLOCK IN, and why they did not used to
       (QBX-222). This case asserted them from frame 0 and said so in as many
       words - "recorded frame 0 IS timeline frame 96000, with no leading
       silence to budget for (the device is not started until the readahead has
       primed - twSpeaker::monitorReadaheadBuffer)". That was an observation
       about the dev box dressed as a guarantee, and it was wrong.

       CAPTURED FRAME 0 IS THE DEVICE START. monitorReadaheadBuffer does start
       the backend only once the readahead reports ready, but that test is
       weaker than "the page at the start position is frozen and valid": when
       the first callback beats it, twAudioEngine's page lookup takes its
       graceful-degradation branch and emits ONE BLOCK OF SILENCE. It leaves no
       trace in the log, because the FOUND and UNDERRUN messages share one
       rate-limited counter (% 500 and % 50) - so the only evidence is the
       recording's own length, one block longer than usual.

       It cost this project two unexplained flakes, two months apart, both of
       them on CORRECT builds and both reported as though the DSP had moved:

           plan/STATE.md, 2026-08-22, section (a)'s 4096-frame window
               0.4823 against [0.5401, 0.5735]
               = 0.556769 * sqrt( (4096 - 1024) / 4096 ) = 0.482176
           CI run 40100984951, 2026-10-24, section (b)'s 2048-frame window
               0.272503 against [0.350061, 0.427853]
               = 0.385377 * sqrt( (2048 - 1024) / 2048 ) = 0.272503

       The second was diagnosed first, and its arithmetic then predicted the
       first to 0.03 % - which is how a flake STATE.md recorded as
       "load-sensitive, not explained" became explained.

       The rule was already written down one verb away, in
       assert-metronome-clicks: "the grid is anchored on the FIRST detected
       onset, deliberately. Capture frame 0 is the DEVICE start, and how many
       blocks of silence the ring took to fill before the first entry was summed
       is a wall-clock quantity of the box". Its tolerance is 1024 frames, one
       device block, and that is the bound used here. Section (c) injects the
       slip and proves all of it.

       WHAT WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED: pinning the recording's FRAME COUNT.
       The stop is a wait-playhead, so the length is "however many blocks were
       pumped before the stop took effect" - 47 blocks here, 48 on the CI run
       that failed. It is a first-rate TRIAGE signal (a one-block difference
       against a local run identifies the slip in seconds) and it would be a
       flaky assertion.

       THE FALSIFICATION, run once by hand. With a debug knob capping K at 4096
       frames (SMARAGD_PREROLL_MAX_FRAMES=4096, a temporary local patch to
       twPluginSlotProcessor::preRoll_nolock, NOT committed), the chased note-on
       restarts the envelope 85 ms before the page instead of 2 s, so env at the
       measured window is exp(-4096/96000) = 0.958 instead of 0.368 and the
       cutoff is 7686 Hz instead of 2963 Hz:

           rms = 0.215076   -> 45 % below the measured value, 39 % below the
                               bottom of the band. Ratio 1.81x.

       Note what that number does and does NOT prove. It proves the reach-back
       sets the absolute level at the locate, which is the property the band
       pins. It does not prove the CAPTURE and the RENDER can drift apart: under
       P3b's recorded double-render debt every instrument page in every consumer
       is produced by the reposition path, so both windows move together (with
       the knob on they both read 0.215076). That is written up in
       instrument_render_determinism.qxa's header and in STATE.md.

       WHY THE FIXTURE IS SHAPED LIKE THIS. A SHORT decay would have converged
       by 2 s and a locate would be free, so nothing would be measured. And the
       note is key 84 rather than a bass note because the discriminator is RMS,
       and the RMS of a saw through a lowpass is dominated by its fundamental
       unless the cutoff sweeps THROUGH it - at 1046.5 Hz the 2963 Hz cutoff
       sits between the second and third harmonic, so the sweep shows up as
       level and not only as timbre.

       THIS CASE reads channel 0 only, and that is now a choice rather than a
       limit: the sink has been as wide as the project since proposal 36 B5, and
       stereo is gated since 2026-08-17 by instrument_stereo_render.qxa. What is
       asserted here is continuity across a locate, which one channel says as
       well as two. -->

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- ===================== (a) the sine: presence ====================== -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <insert-plugin trackPath="0" slotIndex="0"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.sine"
                   name="Smaragd Test Sine" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="0" nOut="2"
                   isInstrument="true"/>
    <assert-instrument-slot trackPath="0" present="1" format="clap"
                            uid="tw.test.clap.sine" mode="DirectGen"
                            state="Active" hasFeed="1"/>
    <insert-midi-clip trackPath="0" timePos="0" duration="7680" name="Pad"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="0" dur="7680" key="60" velocity="100" channel="0"/>

    <!-- ===================== (b) the 303: state ========================== -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <insert-plugin trackPath="1" slotIndex="0"
                   format="tw" uid="tw.native.303"
                   name="Smaragd 303" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="" nIn="0" nOut="1"/>
    <assert-instrument-slot trackPath="1" present="1" format="tw"
                            uid="tw.native.303" mode="MonoSpread"
                            state="Active" hasFeed="1"/>
    <set-plugin-param trackPath="1" slotIndex="0" paramId="0" value="20"/>
    <set-plugin-param trackPath="1" slotIndex="0" paramId="2" value="1.0"/>
    <set-plugin-param trackPath="1" slotIndex="0" paramId="3" value="2.0"/>
    <insert-midi-clip trackPath="1" timePos="0" duration="7680" name="Pad"/>
    <add-note clip="1,0" tick="0" dur="7680" key="84" velocity="90" channel="0"/>

    <!-- ============ (a) locate while STOPPED, then play ================== -->
    <!-- One lane at a time: two instruments playing at once would sum, and
         both assertions below are about ONE voice. -->
    <set-track-mute trackPath="1" muted="1"/>
    <set-locator position="96000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-playhead position="144000" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="30000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="0"/>
    <dump-playback-capture filename="sine_locate.wav" minFrames="32768"/>

    <!-- CLAIM 1, ALIGNMENT AND PRESENCE: the sound starts at the device start,
         within ONE DEVICE BLOCK. That tolerance is not slack - it is the
         house bound assert-metronome-clicks already uses, and it exists
         because the engine may emit one silent block when the first callback
         beats the page it needs (see the header). A chase that produced NO
         voice fails here, by name, instead of surfacing as a mystery RMS. -->
    <assert-audio-onset filename="sine_locate.wav" channel="0"
                        minFrame="0" maxFrame="1024"/>
    <!-- ...and that assertion is LIVE: demanding a late start must refuse on
         an aligned capture. Without this the bound above could be vacuous. -->
    <assert-audio-onset filename="sine_locate.wav" channel="0"
                        minFrame="2048" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- CLAIM 2, PITCH AND LEVEL, measured ONE BLOCK IN so that a tolerated
         slip cannot reach it. The note is THERE (a chase happened) and it is
         at its own pitch. -->
    <assert-audio-frequency filename="sine_locate.wav" startFrame="1024" frameCount="4096"
                            channel="0" minHz="258.626" maxHz="264.626"/>
    <!-- ...and at full level. tw.test.clap.sine is envelope-less with
         amp = velocity, so a held note is exactly velocity/sqrt(2) =
         (100/127)/sqrt(2) = 0.556769; +/-3 % for the 16-bit round trip, the
         same band instrument_sine_render uses. A chase that produced a voice at
         the wrong velocity, or a fade-in, fails here rather than in the
         frequency estimate. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="sine_locate.wav" startFrame="1024" frameCount="4096"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.54007" maxRms="0.57347"/>

    <!-- ============ (b) the reference render, then the same locate ======= -->
    <set-track-mute trackPath="1" muted="0"/>
    <set-track-mute trackPath="0" muted="1"/>

    <render filename="n303_full.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="5.0"/>
    <!-- The reference window, asserted against the SAME literal band as the
         capture below: if the fixture's own level ever moves, this says so
         first, instead of letting a moved reference silently redefine what the
         capture is being compared with. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="n303_full.wav" startFrame="97024" frameCount="2048"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.352754" maxRms="0.431144"/>

    <set-locator position="96000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-playhead position="144000" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="30000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="0"/>
    <dump-playback-capture filename="n303_locate.wav" minFrames="32768"/>

    <assert-audio-onset filename="n303_locate.wav" channel="0"
                        minFrame="0" maxFrame="1024"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="n303_locate.wav" startFrame="1024" frameCount="2048"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.352754" maxRms="0.431144"/>

    <!-- ============ (c) THE SLIP ITSELF, injected and survived ===========
         A third pass, identical to (b), whose dump DELIBERATELY carries one
         block of leading silence (`leadSilenceFrames`, a test-only fault
         injector that logs a warning so a doctored recording can never be
         read as a real one).

         This is the section that makes the whole remedy a gate rather than a
         hope. It states three things that together are the fix:

           1. the onset assertion SEES the slip and still tolerates it;
           2. the moved energy window is UNTOUCHED by it - the number barely
              budges, because 1024 frames is 1 % of a 2-second envelope;
           3. THE OLD FORMULATION WOULD HAVE FAILED. The expectReject on a
              window at frame 0 is the defect this ticket is about, pinned in
              place: `golden * sqrt((2048 - 1024) / 2048)` = 0.272503, which
              is exactly what CI measured on 2026-10-24 and 30 % below the
              band. The same arithmetic on section (a)'s 4096-frame window
              gives 0.482176 against the 0.4823 recorded in plan/STATE.md on
              2026-08-22 - two unexplained flakes, two months apart, one
              cause.
         ================================================================== -->
    <set-locator position="96000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-playhead position="144000" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="30000"/>
    <toggle-playback play="0"/>
    <dump-playback-capture filename="n303_slipped.wav" minFrames="32768"
                           leadSilenceFrames="1024"/>

    <!-- 1. The onset is one block in. The bound is a RANGE and not 1024
         exactly because the detector's running window is 64 frames wide, so
         it fires as soon as the window is mostly signal: MEASURED at 961,
         which is 1024 - (64 - 1) to the frame. The bound is kept loose rather
         than pinned at 961 so that a change to the verb's default window or
         threshold moves it without breaking this case for the wrong reason;
         the verb reports the frame it found either way, which is what makes a
         slip legible instead of mysterious. -->
    <assert-audio-onset filename="n303_slipped.wav" channel="0"
                        minFrame="900" maxFrame="1024"/>

    <!-- ...and maxFrame is LIVE too, which nothing else here would show: on
         the aligned captures the onset is 0 and passes any upper bound, so
         only the slipped file can demonstrate an upper bound refusing. -->
    <assert-audio-onset filename="n303_slipped.wav" channel="0"
                        maxFrame="500" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- 2. ...and the measurement does not care. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="n303_slipped.wav" startFrame="1024" frameCount="2048"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.352754" maxRms="0.431144"/>

    <!-- 3. ...while the window this case used to carry would have failed. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="n303_slipped.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="2048"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.352754" maxRms="0.431144"
                         expectReject="true"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
